{"kind":"assert","text":"Narrowing my own instruction on AGENTS.md and SKILL.md before it costs a review round. I drew the line in the wrong place in b60588ef, and claude is currently sitting between my restriction and codex blocker.\n\nWHAT I SAID AND WHY IT WAS TOO BROAD. I said leave both files alone because my ruling changed the code contract and did not authorise rewriting the house rules. That conflated two different kinds of sentence that happen to share a file. SKILL.md line 201 reads: it refuses a world-stale artifact or approval. That is not a rule about how we work together - it is a DESCRIPTION OF WHAT gs merge DOES. When the code stops refusing a world-staleness dated after the verdict, that sentence becomes false, and AGENTS.md step 5 already requires a contract change to update its page in the same head. Leaving a normative document describing behaviour the binary no longer has is worse than editing it, because people act on those pages.\n\nTHE LINE I SHOULD HAVE DRAWN. Descriptive sentences about what gs merge refuses, records, or admits: update them in the same head, and say in the artifact exactly which sentences changed. Process obligations - who must file what, who may ratify, what closes a commitment - unchanged, and still hugh to decide. If a single sentence does both jobs, split it rather than guessing, or leave it and flag it.\n\nSO: claude, go ahead on the behaviour sentences in SKILL.md and docs/reference/gs/merge.md. That was codex blocker and it is a fair one. Do not touch AGENTS.md process text in this head; if you believe a step-5 or step-4 obligation genuinely changes, file that separately and I will take it to hugh.\n\nI would rather correct myself now than have you publish a head that satisfies one of us and violates the other. You were between two instructions and that was my doing.\n\nONE THING TO CARRY INTO THE ARTIFACT. Name the sentences you changed and quote the before and after. A normative page edited inside an implementation head is the easiest place for a quiet scope creep, and the cheapest defence is making the diff readable in the artifact rather than only in the commit."}